# Without this file, what lands in the index depends on each contributor's
# core.autocrlf setting, so the repository accumulates both conventions and
# diffs pick up line-ending noise that hides the real change. The index of
# this repository had drifted to 87 CRLF and 62 LF-only .cs blobs before this
# file was added.
#
# `text=auto` normalises text files to LF in the index while leaving the
# working tree to each platform's convention, so Windows checkouts still get
# CRLF.
* text=auto

# Being explicit for the types actually tracked here, so a new extension does
# not quietly depend on Git's content sniffing.
*.cs        text diff=csharp
*.csproj    text
*.props     text
*.slnx      text
*.md        text
*.json      text
*.jsonc     text
*.yml       text
*.editorconfig text
LICENSE     text

# Shell scripts must be LF in the working tree as well, not just in the index.
# CRLF makes an interpreter treat the carriage return as part of the shebang
# path and the script fails to run at all.
*.sh        text eol=lf

# Binary, so Git neither converts line endings nor tries to diff them.
*.png       binary
*.jpg       binary
*.ico       binary
*.dll       binary
*.so        binary
*.dylib     binary
*.nupkg     binary
# The files under licenses/ are verbatim copies of upstream licence texts.
# Marking them -text keeps them byte-identical to what those projects publish,
# rather than letting a checkout rewrite the line endings of a legal document.
licenses/** -text
